13	 * PENDING} Transfer to hold the request while its Checks are outstanding.
14	 *
15	 * <p><b>The order inside {@link #reserve} is the correctness argument</b>, and it is the
16	 * reason the three statements are not interchangeable:
17	 *
18	 * <ol>
19	 * <li>refuse a self-Transfer, which the request decides on its own;
20	 * <li>lock both Accounts, ascending by ID, which {@link AccountLocking} owns;
21	 * <li>refuse a cross-Currency Transfer, at the first moment both Currencies are known;
22	 * <li><em>then</em> read the source Account's Available Balance and test the amount against
23	 * it;
24	 * <li>write the reservation, the Transfer, and the Check Ledger the Transfer will be
25	 * settled or rejected against.
26	 * </ol>
27	 *
28	 * <p>Because the check follows the lock, the figure it tests against cannot be invalidated
29	 * between reading it and acting on it: any other transaction that would spend the same
30	 * Available Balance is waiting on the row until this one commits, and then reads what this
31	 * one wrote. Checking first and locking second would compile, pass every single-threaded
32	 * test, and overdraw under load.
33	 *
34	 * <p>Nothing here saves the source Account, and that is not an omission. The Accounts come
35	 * back from {@link AccountLocking} attached to this transaction's persistence context, so
36	 * raising the Reserved Amount <em>is</em> the write, flushed on commit alongside the
37	 * Transfer. There is no other way for this slice to do it either: design decision 30 keeps
38	 * {@code AccountRepository} package-private, and locking is the whole of what {@code
39	 * accounts} exports.
40	 *
41	 * <p>The Exchange Rate is deliberately not fetched here. Design decision 4 puts it in a
42	 * phase of its own with no transaction open and no locks held, which is what makes
43	 * pessimistic locking affordable at all; {@code LockedPathTouchesOnlyTheDatabaseTest} names
44	 * this class and fails if anything it can reach would wait on a provider.
45	 *
46	 * <p><b>Same-Currency Transfers only, and refused rather than written.</b> Both amounts on
47	 * the Transfer are denominated by the source Account, so a Transfer between two Currencies
48	 * has no honest figure for the credited side. Step 3 refuses it; it does not sit later,
49	 * because {@link Account#reserve} <em>is</em> the write and a refusal after it has already
50	 * recorded the reservation it meant to prevent. What the refusal names is a capability this
51	 * service lacks rather than a rule it keeps, so ticket 26 gives the credited side an
52	 * Exchange Rate of its own and deletes the check along with {@link
53	 * CrossCurrencyTransferNotSupportedException}.
54	 */
55	@Service
56	class FundsReservation {
57	
58		private final AccountLocking accounts;
59	
60		private final TransferRepository transfers;
61	
62		private final CheckLedger ledger;
63	
64		FundsReservation(AccountLocking accounts, TransferRepository transfers, CheckLedger ledger) {
65			this.accounts = accounts;
66			this.transfers = transfers;
